(a) Operation of motorized mobility devices:
(1) Motorized mobility devices shall not be operated by any person under 18 years of age.
(2) Riding of motorized mobility devices is prohibited on sidewalks and on streets with a designated speed of 36 miles per hour or more.
(3) Motorized mobility devices are to be ridden only in bicycle lanes and on bicycle paths, or on streets with a designated speed of 35 miles per hour or less, if no bicycle lane or bicycle path is available.
(4) Motorized mobility devices may be ridden upon any roadway with a designated speed of 35 miles per hour or less when no bicycle lane or bicycle path is available; provided that such operation is as near to the right side of the roadway as practicable.
(5) Motorized mobility devices are to stay to the right of street lanes, except when:
a. Turning left;
b. Avoiding hazards to safe operation; or
c. The lane is too narrow to share safely with a motor vehicle.
(6) Motorized mobility devices are to offer the right-of-way to bicycles and electric assisted bicycles in bicycle lanes and on bicycle paths.
(7) No person operating a motorized mobility device upon a roadway, bicycle lane or bicycle path shall ride more than two abreast.
(8) Any person operating a motorized mobility device upon a roadway, bicycle lane or bicycle path shall ride in the same direction as traffic.
(9) Motorized mobility devices shall not be operated between the hours of 9:00 p.m. and 6:00 a.m.
(10) Motorized mobility devices shall not be operated at a speed greater than 15 miles per hour.
(11) No operator shall use motorized mobility devices to carry more than one person at a time.
(12) No operator shall take hold of or hang on to any automobile or other vehicle while operating a motorized mobility device.
(13) Any person operating a motorized mobility device may signal a right turn with said person's right arm and hand extended horizontally or may signal a left turn with said person's left hand and arm extended upward.
(14) No person operating a motorized mobility device shall carry any package, bundle, or other article which prevents said person from keeping both hands on the handles or otherwise properly and safely operating the device.
(15) No person shall use a stand-alone electronic device or wireless telecommunication device, as those terms are defined by Chapter 6 of Title 40 of the Official Code of Georgia, while operating a motorized mobility device.
(16) Persons operating motorized mobility devices shall comply with the Georgia Uniform Rules of the Road (Chapter 6 of Title 40 of the Official Code of Georgia).
(b) Parking of motorized mobility devices:
(1) Operators shall only park motorized mobility devices upright on hard surfaces in the landscape zone of the sidewalk, beside a public bicycle rack or in another area specifically designated for bicycle or motorized mobility device parking, in such a manner as to afford the least obstruction of pedestrian traffic and provide a minimum of five feet clear for pedestrians.
(2) Operators shall not park motorized mobility devices in the street except in street parking spaces specifically authorized by signage for use by motorized mobility devices.
(3) Except when necessary to avoid conflict with other traffic or to comply with the law or the directions of law enforcement or official traffic-control devices, no individual shall stop, stand, or park a motorized mobility device:
a. Upon any portion of the sidewalk that is not the landscape zone;
b. Within an intersection;
c. On a crosswalk;
d. Alongside or opposite any street excavation or obstruction where such stopping, standing, or parking would obstruct traffic;
e. On any railroad tracks;
f. In a manner that obstructs access to any public transit bus stop;
g. At any place where official signs prohibit stopping, standing, or parking motorized mobility devices;
h. On any drain grate or utility or sewer covering;
i. In any designated loading zone;
j. In any driveway, vehicle travel lane, bicycle lane, or bicycle path;
k. In a manner that obstructs pedestrian or wheelchair access to: buildings and establishments; street features that require pedestrian access, including, but not limited to, benches, parking meters, parking pay stations, and crosswalk signal buttons; and, accessibility and safety features, including, but not limited to, curb ramps, wheelchair ramps, ramp landings, and handrails;
l. In a manner that obscures any traffic-control devices or directional or other official signs and notices; or
m. In a manner that obstructs access to emergency service infrastructure.
(4) The city may remove, reposition, rebalance or impound any motorized mobility devices as needed to address immediate hazards and as provided in section 98-187 (e) of the Code of Ordinances and charge the company and/or operator for any costs to the city.
(5) If a motorized mobility device is parked in a manner that impedes the flow of vehicular or pedestrian traffic or poses a threat to safety, any person shall be authorized to reposition or move such device in order to remove such impediment or threat, provided that such action performed is not more than necessary to reposition such vehicle to a place that no longer impedes traffic or poses a threat to safety and is not performed recklessly.
